ne must have exactly one point from column 1. So B1 must be paired with either A3 or A4.\n\nCheck A3=(1,3) with B1=(2,1): slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. So L3: (1,3)-(2,1). This line passes through (3,-1) not in T_4. So L3 covers A3 and B1.\n\nNow we have A4=(1,4) left for L4. B points are all covered now (B1,B2,B3). So L4 will only have A4 from column 1, and maybe can cover points from columns 3 or 4.\n\nNow we need to cover column 3 points C1=(3,1), C2=(3,2), and D=(4,1). We have lines L1, L2, L3, L4. L1 currently passes through (1,1) and (2,2). It can also intersect column 3 at some point (3,y) if the line passes through (3,y). Let's compute L1 equation: slope 1, passes through (1,1): y = x. So at x=3, y=3 => point (3,3) not in T_4. So L1 cannot cover any column 3 point.\n\nL2: passes through (1,2) and (2,3). Equation: slope 1, passes through (1,2): y = x+1. At x=3, y=4 => (3,4) not in T_4. So L2 cannot cover column 3 points.\n\nL3: passes through (1,3) and (2,1). Slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2. Equation: y - 3 = -2 (x - 1) => y = -2x + 5. At x=3, y = -6 + 5 = -1 => (3,-1) not in T_4. So L3 cannot cover column 3 points.\n\nL4: currently only has (1,4). We can choose L4 to pass through (1,4) and maybe (3,2) or (3,1) or (4,1). Let's see possibilities.\n\n- L4 through (1,4) and (3,2): slope = (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So cannot.\n\n- L4 through (1,4) and (3,1): slope = (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5, allowed. So L4: (1,4)-(3,1). Equation: slope -1.5, passes through (1,4): y - 4 = -1.5 (x - 1) => y = -1.5 x + 5.5. At x=4, y = -6 + 5.5 = -0.5, not (4,1). So L4 covers (1,4) and (3,1). Good.\n\nNow we have covered (3,1) via L4. Remaining points: (3,2) and (4,1). We have no lines left (all 4 lines used). So we cannot cover (3,2) and (4,1). Could we adjust assignments to cover them? Perhaps we could have L4 cover (4,1) instead of (3,1). Let's try L4 through (1,4) and (4,1): slope = (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed. So cannot.\n\nCould we have L3 cover (3,2) instead of (2,1)? L3 currently passes through (1,3) and (2,1). Could we change L3 to pass through (1,3) and (3,2)? Slope = (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5, allowed. Then L3 would be (1,3)-(3,2). Does it also pass through (2,1)? No, but we need to cover (2,1) somewhere else. B1 = (2,1) is currently uncovered. Could we assign B1 to L4? L4 currently has (1,4). Could we have L4 pass through (1,4) and (2,1)? Slope = (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, allowed. So L4: (1,4)-(2,1). Then L4 also passes through (3,-2) not needed. So B1 covered by L4. Then we need to cover (3,1) and (4,1) and (3,2). L1 and L2 cannot cover column 3 points. L3 covers (3,2). L4 covers (2,1) and (1,4). So we still need to cover (3,1) and (4,1). No lines left. So fails.\n\nMaybe we need to assign some column 2 points differently to free up a line for column 3 and 4 points. Let's try a different assignment.\n\nWe have 4 lines, each must have a point from column 1. So we have 4 lines L1-L4 each with A_i. We need to assign B points to some of them, and also assign C and D points. Since we have 4 lines, we can assign at most 4 points from column 2 (but there are only 3). So one line will not have a column 2 point. Similarly, we have 2 points in column 3, which can be assigned to at most 2 lines. And column 4 point can be assigned to at most 1 line. So we need to assign these points to lines such that each line's points are collinear.\n\nWe need to find a set of 4 lines that each contain a point from column 1, and collectively cover all points. This is like a system of equations. Let's try to find a systematic approach.\n\nWe can treat each line as determined by two points (since we have at most 4 points per line). Since we have 10 points, each line must contain at least 2 points (except maybe one line could contain only one point). But we need to cover all points.\n\nWe can try to find a solution using a computer search for n=4, k=4. But we can reason manually.\n\nAlternatively, we can try to see if there is a known result: For covering a triangular grid of points with lines not parallel to axes or diagonal, the maximum number of sunny lines needed is 3. The solution suggests that k≥4 is impossible. But we need to verify if there is a counterexample for n=4, k=4.\n\nLet's try to find a configuration for n=4 with k=4. Since all lines must be sunny, we cannot use vertical/horizontal/diagonal lines. So each line must have slope not 0, ∞, -1. Let's try to find a set of 4 lines that cover all points.\n\nWe can try to use lines that go through many points. For example, line through (1,1) and (2,2) and (3,3) and (4,4) would be slope 1, but (4,4) is not in T_4 because a+b=8 >5. So line (1,1)-(2,2)-(3,3) includes (3,3) which is not in T_4 (since a+b=6 >5). So cannot use that line.\n\nLine through (1,2)-(2,3)-(3,4) includes (3,4) not in T_4. So not helpful.\n\nLine through (1,3)-(2,2)-(3,1) is slope -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (1,4)-(2,3)-(3,2)-(4,1) is slope -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (1,1)-(3,2) slope = (2-1)/(3-1) = 1/2, allowed. It also passes through (2,1.5) not integer. So line L1: (1,1)-(3,2). It covers (1,1) and (3,2). Good.\n\nLine through (1,2)-(3,1) slope = (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2, allowed. L2: (1,2)-(3,1). Covers (1,2) and (3,1).\n\nLine through (1,3)-(2,1) slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. L3: (1,3)-(2,1). Covers (1,3) and (2,1).\n\nLine through (1,4)-(2,2) slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. L4: (1,4)-(2,2). Covers (1,4) and (2,2).\n\nNow we have covered points: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4) all covered. (2,1) covered by L3. (2,2) covered by L4. (2,3) not covered. (3,1) covered by L2. (3,2) covered by L1. (4,1) not covered. So we still need to cover (2,3) and (4,1). We have used all 4 lines. So fails.\n\nWe could try to adjust lines to cover (2,3) and (4,1). Perhaps we can have a line that covers (2,3) and (4,1). Slope = (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So cannot pair (2,3) with (4,1). Could pair (2,3) with (1,2) slope = (3-2)/(2-1) = 1, allowed. So we could have a line L5: (1,2)-(2,3). But we only have 4 lines. So we need to incorporate (2,3) into one of the existing lines. Let's see if we can modify L2: currently L2 is (1,2)-(3,1). Could we replace (3,1) with (2,3)? That would be line through (1,2)-(2,3) slope 1, allowed. That line would be L2': (1,2)-(2,3). It would not cover (3,1). So we need to cover (3,1) elsewhere. Maybe L1 could cover (3,1) as well? L1 currently is (1,1)-(3,2). Could we adjust L1 to cover (3,1) as well? Let's see line through (1,1)-(3,1) slope = (1-1)/(3-1) = 0, horizontal, not allowed. So cannot.\n\nMaybe we can have L3 cover (3,1) and (2,3) together? Let's see line through (2,3)-(3,1) slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, allowed. So line L3': (2,3)-(3,1). Then we need to cover (1,2) somewhere else. Could be covered by L2 (1,2)-(2,1) maybe? Let's see line through (1,2)-(2,1) slope = (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. So cannot.\n\nAlternatively, line through (1,2)-(4,1) slope = (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3, allowed. So we could have a line covering (1,2) and (4,1). That would be L2' maybe. Then we need to cover (2,3) and (3,1) with another line. As we saw, line (2,3)-(3,1) slope -2, allowed. So we could have L3' = (2,3)-(3,1). Then we need to cover (2,2) and (1,4) maybe with L4. Let's see line (1,4)-(2,2) slope = -2, allowed. So L4 = (1,4)-(2,2). Then we need to cover (1,1),(1,3),(2,1) and maybe (3,2). Let's see L1 = (1,1)-(3,2) slope = (2-1)/(3-1) = 1/2, allowed. L1 covers (1,1) and (3,2). L5 = (1,3)-(2,1) slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. But we have only 4 lines, we already used L1, L2', L3', L4. That's 4 lines. We still have (1,3) and (2,1) uncovered. So fails.\n\nThus seems difficult to cover all points with 4 sunny lines for n=4. But we need to be sure.\n\nWe can try a systematic approach: Since each line can have at most one point per column, we need to assign each column's points to lines. For column 1 (4 points), each line must have exactly one of them. So each line L_i has a distinct A_i. For column 2 (3 points), we need to assign them to 3 of the lines. For column 3 (2 points), assign to 2 lines. For column 4 (1 point), assign to 1 line.\n\nThus each line will have a set of points: one from column 1, maybe one from column 2, maybe one from column 3, maybe one from column 4. The total number of points per line can be up to 4.\n\nWe need to find a set of 4 lines such that each line passes through its assigned points. This is a system of constraints: For each line, the points assigned must be collinear.\n\nWe can try to assign points to lines in a way that yields collinearity.\n\nOne approach: assign each line to have exactly one point from each column (so each line has 4 points). Since there are 4 lines, that would cover all points (4+3+2+1 = 10 points). But column 2 has only 3 points, so one line would have no point from column 2. Column 3 has 2 points, so two lines would have no point from column 3. Column 4 has 1 point, so three lines would have no point from column 4. So the distribution of points per line would be something like: some lines have 4 points, some have 3, some have 2, etc.\n\nBut we need to find collinear sets.\n\nLet's try to assign points to lines as follows:\n\n- L1: (1,1) from col1, (2,2) from col2, (3,3?) not in T_4, (4,4?) not in T_4. So L1 would have only (1,1) and (2,2). That's okay.\n\n- L2: (1,2) from col1, (2,3) from col2, (3,4?) not in T_4. So L2 has (1,2),(2,3).\n\n- L3: (1,3) from col1, (2,1) from col2, (3,2) from col3, (4,3?) not in T_4. So L3 has (1,3),(2,1),(3,2). Are these collinear? Let's check slopes: (1,3)-(2,1) slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2. (2,1)-(3,2) slope = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1. Not same, so not collinear.\n\n- L4: (1,4) from col1, (2,? maybe none), (3,1) from col3, (4,1) from col4. Check collinearity: (1,4)-(3,1) slope = (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5. (3,1)-(4,1) slope = 0, not same. So not collinear.\n\nThus this assignment fails.\n\nWe need to find a set of collinear triples or quadruples.\n\nPerhaps we can use lines that go through points across columns with slopes that produce integer coordinates. For example, line through (1,1) and (3,2) slope 1/2, passes through (2,1.5) not integer. So not helpful.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (4,1) slope 0, not allowed.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (4,2) slope (2-1)/(4-1) = 1/3, allowed, but (4,2) not in T_4 (since a+b=6 >5). So not.\n\nLine through (1,2) and (4,1) slope -1/3, allowed. That line passes through (2,5/3) not integer, (3,4/3) not integer. So not helpful.\n\nLine through (1,3) and (4,1) slope (1-3)/(4-1) = -2/3, allowed. Passes through (2,7/3) not integer, (3,5/3) not integer.\n\nLine through (1,4) and (4,1) slope -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (2,1) and (4,1) slope 0, not allowed.\n\nLine through (2,1) and (3,2) slope (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, allowed. So line L: (2,1)-(3,2). It also passes through (1,0) not in T_4. So L covers (2,1) and (3,2). Good.\n\nLine through (2,2) and (3,1) slope (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (2,2) and (4,1) slope (1-2)/(4-2) = -1/2, allowed. So line M: (2,2)-(4,1). It also passes through (3,1.5) not integer.\n\nLine through (2,3) and (3,1) slope (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, allowed. So line N: (2,3)-(3,1). It also passes through (1,5) not in T_4.\n\nLine through (1,1) and (2,3) slope (3-1)/(2-1) = 2, allowed. So line O: (1,1)-(2,3). It also passes through (3,5) not in T_4.\n\nLine through (1,2) and (2,1) slope (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (1,2) and (3,2) slope (2-2)/(3-1) = 0, not allowed.\n\nLine through (1,2) and (4,1) slope -1/3, allowed.\n\nLine through (1,3) and (2,2) slope (2-3)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (1,3) and (2,3) slope 0, not allowed.\n\nLine through (1,3) and (3,1) slope -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (1,4) and (2,1) slope (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, allowed.\n\nLine through (1,4) and (3,2) slope (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (1,4) and (4,1) slope -1, not allowed.\n\nThus many possible lines.\n\nWe need to find a set of 4 lines that cover all points. Let's try to systematically search manually.\n\nWe can think of each line as a set of points. Since each line can have at most one point per column, the maximum number of points a line can have is 4 (if it has a point in each column). But many lines will have only 2 or 3 points.\n\nWe need to cover 10 points with 4 lines, so average 2.5 points per line. So some lines will have 3 points, some 2.\n\nWe can try to find a line that covers 3 points. For example, line through (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) would be slope 1, but (3,3) not in T_4. So not.\n\nLine through (1,2),(2,3),(3,4) not in T_4.\n\nLine through (1,3),(2,2),(3,1) slope -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1) slope -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (1,1),(3,2) slope 1/2, only 2 points.\n\nLine through (1,2),(3,1) slope -1/2, 2 points.\n\nLine through (1,3),(3,2) slope -1/2? Actually (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2, allowed. So line through (1,3)-(3,2) slope -0.5, also passes through (2,2.5) not integer.\n\nLine through (1,4),(3,2) slope -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (2,1),(4,1) slope 0, not allowed.\n\nLine through (2,1),(3,2) slope 1, allowed. So line L1: (2,1)-(3,2). Could also include (1,0) not in T_4.\n\nLine through (2,2),(4,1) slope -0.5, allowed. L2: (2,2)-(4,1). Could also include (3,1.5) not integer.\n\nLine through (2,3),(4,1) slope (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (2,3),(3,1) slope -2, allowed. L3: (2,3)-(3,1). Could also include (1,5) not in T_4.\n\nLine through (1,1),(2,3) slope 2, allowed. L4: (1,1)-(2,3). Could also include (3,5) not in T_4.\n\nNow we have lines L1: (2,1),(3,2); L2: (2,2),(4,1); L3: (2,3),(3,1); L4: (1,1),(2,3). Wait L4 uses (2,3) again, conflict: each point must be covered at least once, but can be covered multiple times? The condition is \"the point (a,b) is on at least one of the lines\". So points can be covered multiple times, that's okay. So we can have overlapping coverage. However, we need to cover all points. Let's see which points are covered:\n\n- (1,1): covered by L4.\n- (1,2): not covered yet.\n- (1,3): not covered.\n- (1,4): not covered.\n- (2,1): covered by L1.\n- (2,2): covered by L2.\n- (2,3): covered by L3 and L4.\n- (3,1): covered by L3.\n- (3,2): covered by L1.\n- (4,1): covered by L2.\n\nRemaining uncovered: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4). We have used 4 lines already, cannot add more. So fails.\n\nWe could try to adjust lines to also cover column 1 points. Since each line must have a point from column 1 (since we have 4 lines and 4 points in column 1). So each line must include one of (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). In our current set, only L4 includes (1,1). L1, L2, L3 have no column 1 points. So we need to modify them.\n\nWe can try to replace L1 with a line that includes (1,1) and (2,1) maybe? That's vertical, not allowed. (1,1)-(2,2) slope 1, allowed. So L1': (1,1)-(2,2). That covers (1,1) and (2,2). But (2,2) already covered by L2. That's okay. L1' also covers (3,3) not in T_4. So L1' covers (1,1) and (2,2). L2 currently is (2,2)-(4,1). That also covers (2,2). Could keep L2.\n\nNow we need to cover (1,2),(1,3),(1,4). Let's try to assign L2 to include (1,2). L2 currently is (2,2)-(4,1). Could we change L2 to include (1,2)? Let's see line through (1,2) and (4,1) slope = (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3, allowed. So L2': (1,2)-(4,1). That covers (1,2) and (4,1). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover (1,3) and (1,4). We have L3 and L4 left. L3 currently is (2,3)-(3,1). Could we modify L3 to include (1,3)? Let's see line through (1,3) and (3,1) slope = (1-3)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So cannot. Could we include (1,3) and (2,3)? That's horizontal, not allowed. Could include (1,3) and (2,1) slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. So line through (1,3)-(2,1) slope -2. That line also passes through (3,-1) not needed. So L3': (1,3)-(2,1). That covers (1,3) and (2,1). Good.\n\nNow L4 currently is (1,1)-(2,3). But (1,1) already covered by L1', but that's okay. However, we need to cover (1,4). L4 could be (1,4)-(2,? maybe 2,2?) Let's see line through (1,4)-(2,2) slope = (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. So L4': (1,4)-(2,2). That covers (1,4) and (2,2). But (2,2) already covered by L1' and L2'? Actually L2' covers (4,1) and (1,2). L1' covers (1,1) and (2,2). So (2,2) is covered. That's okay.\n\nNow let's list coverage:\n\n- L1': (1,1),(2,2)\n- L2': (1,2),(4,1)\n- L3': (1,3),(2,1)\n- L4': (1,4),(2,2) (duplicate (2,2))\n\nPoints covered:\n\nColumn 1: (1,1) L1', (1,2) L2', (1,3) L3', (1,4) L4' => all covered.\n\nColumn 2: (2,1) L3', (2,2) L1' and L4', (2,3) not covered yet.\n\nColumn 3: (3,1) not covered, (3,2) not covered.\n\nColumn 4: (4,1) L2'.\n\nWe still need to cover (2,3),(3,1),(3,2). We have no lines left. So fails.\n\nThus this assignment fails.\n\nWe could try to incorporate (2,3) into one of the lines that already has a column 1 point. For example, L1' could be (1,1)-(2,3) slope = (3-1)/(2-1) = 2, allowed. That would cover (1,1) and (2,3). Then we need to cover (2,2) elsewhere. Let's try a new assignment.\n\nGoal: assign each line to have a column 1 point and possibly other points.\n\nWe have 4 lines L1-L4, each with a distinct column 1 point: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). We need to cover all other points: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). That's 6 points. Each line can cover at most one point from each of columns 2,3,4. So total capacity for covering column 2 points across 4 lines is at most 4 (but we have 3 points). Column 3 points capacity at most 4 (we have 2). Column 4 capacity at most 4 (we have 1). So capacity is enough. We need to assign each of the 6 points to some line such that each line's assigned points are collinear.\n\nWe can treat this as a matching problem: assign each point to a line such that each line's set of points (including its column 1 point) are collinear.\n\nLet's denote the column 1 points as A1=(1,1), A2=(1,2), A3=(1,3), A4=(1,4). The other points: B1=(2,1), B2=(2,2), B3=(2,3), C1=(3,1), C2=(3,2), D1=(4,1).\n\nWe need to assign each B, C, D to some line L_i (i=1..4). Each line can have at most one B, at most one C, at most one D. So each line can have up to 4 points (including A_i). We need to assign all B1,B2,B3,C1,C2,D1.\n\nWe need to find collinear sets: For each line L_i, the set {A_i, maybe B_j, maybe C_k, maybe D1} must be collinear.\n\nThus we need to find for each i a line passing through A_i and possibly other points.\n\nWe can try to assign D1 (4,1) to some line. Which A_i can be collinear with D1? Let's compute slopes:\n\n- Slope from A1 (1,1) to D1 (4,1) = (1-1)/(4-1) = 0, horizontal, not allowed. So cannot assign D1 to line with A1.\n\n- Slope from A2 (1,2) to D1 (4,1) = (1-2)/(4-1) = -1/3, allowed. So line L2 could be (1,2)-(4,1). That line also passes through (2,5/3) not integer, (3,4/3) not integer. So only those two points.\n\n- Slope from A3 (1,3) to D1 (4,1) = (1-3)/(4-1) = -2/3, allowed. So line L3 could be (1,3)-(4,1). That line passes through (2,7/3) not integer, (3,5/3) not integer.\n\n- Slope from A4 (1,4) to D1 (4,1) = (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, not allowed. So cannot assign D1 to line with A4.\n\nThus D1 can be assigned to L2 or L3.\n\nNow assign B points.\n\nConsider B1 = (2,1). Which A_i can be collinear with B1 with a sunny line?\n\n- A1 (1,1) to B1 (2,1): slope 0, not allowed.\n- A2 (1,2) to B1 (2,1): slope (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed.\n- A3 (1,3) to B1 (2,1): slope (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. So line L3 could be (1,3)-(2,1). That line also passes through (3,-1) not needed.\n- A4 (1,4) to B1 (2,1): slope (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, allowed. So line L4 could be (1,4)-(2,1).\n\nThus B1 can be assigned to L3 or L4.\n\nConsider B2 = (2,2). Slopes:\n\n- A1 (1,1) to B2: slope (2-1)/(2-1) = 1, allowed. So L1 could be (1,1)-(2,2).\n- A2 (1,2) to B2: slope (2-2)/(2-1) = 0, not allowed.\n- A3 (1,3) to B2: slope (2-3)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed.\n- A4 (1,4) to B2: slope (2-4)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. So L4 could be (1,4)-(2,2).\n\nThus B2 can be assigned to L1 or L4.\n\nConsider B3 = (2,3). Slopes:\n\n- A1 (1,1) to B3: slope (3-1)/(2-1) = 2, allowed. So L1 could be (1,1)-(2,3).\n- A2 (1,2) to B3: slope (3-2)/(2-1) = 1, allowed. So L2 could be (1,2)-(2,3).\n- A3 (1,3) to B3: slope (3-3)/(2-1) = 0, not allowed.\n- A4 (1,4) to B3: slope (3-4)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus B3 can be assigned to L1 or L2.\n\nNow C points:\n\nC1 = (3,1). Slopes:\n\n- A1 (1,1) to C1: slope (1-1)/(3-1) = 0, not allowed.\n- A2 (1,2) to C1: slope (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5, allowed. So L2 could be (1,2)-(3,1).\n- A3 (1,3) to C1: slope (1-3)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n- A4 (1,4) to C1: slope (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5, allowed. So L4 could be (1,4)-(3,1).\n\nThus C1 can be assigned to L2 or L4.\n\nC2 = (3,2). Slopes:\n\n- A1 (1,1) to C2: slope (2-1)/(3-1) = 1/2 = 0.5, allowed. So L1 could be (1,1)-(3,2).\n- A2 (1,2) to C2: slope (2-2)/(3-1) = 0, not allowed.\n- A3 (1,3) to C2: slope (2-3)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5, allowed. So L3 could be (1,3)-(3,2).\n- A4 (1,4) to C2: slope (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus C2 can be assigned to L1 or L3.\n\nNow D1 = (4,1) we already considered: can be assigned to L2 or L3.\n\nNow we need to assign each point to a line such that each line gets at most one point from each column (i.e., at most one B, at most one C, at most one D). Also each line must have at least its A_i.\n\nWe need to find a matching.\n\nLet's try to assign D1 to L2 (since L2 can have D1). Then L2 will have A2 (1,2) and D1 (4,1). L2 can also have at most one B and one C. Let's see which B and C can be assigned to L2.\n\nB3 can be assigned to L2 (since (1,2)-(2,3) slope 1). B2 cannot (slope 0). B1 cannot (slope -1). So B3 is possible for L2. C1 can be assigned to L2 (since (1,2)-(3,1) slope -0.5). C2 cannot (slope 0). So L2 could have B3 and C1 as well. But we need to ensure each line has at most one B and one C. So L2 could have A2, B3, C1, D1. That's 4 points, all collinear? Wait, are (1,2),(2,3),(3,1),(4,1) collinear? Let's check: (1,2)-(2,3) slope = 1. (2,3)-(3,1) slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, not same. So they are not collinear. So we cannot assign b