ine. So this approach fails.\n\nThus we need a different arrangement.\n\nMaybe we can use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-4 (n-4 lines). Then we have 4 columns left: n-3, n-2, n-1, n. We have 4 lines left: we need exactly 2 sunny lines, and the other 2 lines can be non-sunny (vertical/horizontal/slope -1). So we have flexibility: we can use a horizontal line and a slope -1 line, plus 2 sunny lines. That gives total n lines: (n-4) vertical + 1 horizontal + 1 slope -1 + 2 sunny = n. Let's see if we can cover the points in columns n-3 to n with these 4 lines.\n\nColumns:\n\n- Column n-3: points (n-3,1), (n-3,2), (n-3,3), (n-3,4) (since a+b ≤ n+1 => b ≤ n+1 - (n-3) = 4). So 4 points.\n\n- Column n-2: points (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3) (b ≤ 3). So 3 points.\n\n- Column n-1: points (n-1,1), (n-1,2). So 2 points.\n\n- Column n: point (n,1). So 1 point.\n\nTotal points in these columns: 4+3+2+1 = 10 points.\n\nWe have 4 lines to cover them. Each line can cover multiple points if they are collinear. We need to find a configuration of 2 sunny lines, 1 horizontal line, 1 slope -1 line that covers all 10 points.\n\nWe can try to use the horizontal line y=1 to cover all points with b=1: (n-3,1), (n-2,1), (n-1,1), (n,1). That's 4 points.\n\nWe can use the slope -1 line y = -x + c to cover points on the hypotenuse a+b = n+1: those are (a, n+1-a) for a from 1 to n. In particular, among the remaining columns, we have points (n-3,4), (n-2,3), (n-1,2), (n,1). But (n,1) is already covered by y=1. So slope -1 line y = -x + (n+1) passes through (n-3,4), (n-2,3), (n-1,2), (n,1). So that line covers (n-3,4), (n-2,3), (n-1,2). Good. So far we have covered:\n\n- y=1: (n-3,1), (n-2,1), (n-1,1), (n,1)\n- slope -1 line: (n-3,4), (n-2,3), (n-1,2)\n\nRemaining points: (n-3,2), (n-3,3), (n-2,2). That's three points left.\n\nWe have 2 sunny lines left to cover these three points. Can we cover them with two sunny lines? Let's see.\n\nPoints: (n-3,2), (n-3,3), (n-2,2). We need to cover them with two