hese points. For example, line y = -x + (n+2) passes through (n-3,5), (n-2,4), (n-1,3), (n,2). But those points are not in the set (b > allowed). So not helpful.\n\nMaybe we can use a vertical line x = n-3 as a non-sunny line. That would cover (n-3,1), (n-3,2), (n-3,3), (n-3,4). But we already have vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-4. So we could replace one of those vertical lines with x = n-3, but we need to cover column n-3 anyway. Actually we omitted vertical lines for columns n-3,...,n. So we could include x = n-3 as a vertical line (non-sunny). Then we have vertical line covering all points in column n-3. That would cover (n-3,1),(n-3,2),(n-3,3),(n-3,4). Then we need to cover columns n-2, n-1, n with the remaining 3 lines: we have 2 sunny lines and 1 non-sunny line left (maybe slope -1). So we have vertical line x=n-3, slope -1 line y = -x + (n+1) covering (n-2,3),(n-1,2),(n,1). Then we need to cover (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-1,1),(n,1?) Actually (n,1) is covered by slope -1 line. So left points: (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-1,1). That's three points. We have 2 sunny lines left. Can we cover these three points with two sunny lines? Let's see: Pair (n-2,1) with (n-1,1): horizontal, not allowed. Pair (n-2,1) with (n-2,2): vertical, not allowed. Pair (n-1,1) with (n-2,2): slope = (2-1)/(n-2 - (n-1)) = 1/(-1) = -1, not allowed. So we cannot cover them with sunny lines. So this fails.\n\nThus we need a different arrangement.\n\nMaybe we can use a horizontal line y=2 to cover (n-2,2),(n-1,2),(n-3,2). But we need to cover (n-2,1),(n-1,1),(n,1) and (n-3,1),(n-3,3),(n-3,4). We could use a slope -1 line to cover (n-3,4),(n-2,3),(n-1,2),(n,1). Then we have left (n-3,1),(n-3,2),(n-3,3),(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-1,1). That's 6 points. We have 2 sunny lines left (since we used vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-4, plus horizontal y=2, plus slope -1 line). Actually we used vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-4 (n-4 lines). Then we used horizontal y=2 (1 line). Then slope -1 line (1 line). That's n-